==English==

===Etymology===
[[char]] + [[woman]].

===Noun===
{{en-noun|charwomen}}

# {{context|chiefly|British|lang=en}} A woman employed to do [[housework]].
#*{{quote-book|year=1918|author={{w|W. B. Maxwell}}|chapter=17
|title=[http://openlibrary.org/works/OL1097634W The Mirror and the Lamp]
|passage=This time was most dreadful for Lilian. Thrown on her own resources and almost penniless, she maintained herself and paid the rent of a wretched room near the hospital by working as a '''charwoman''', sempstress, anything.}}

Note: In the nineteenth century, the charlady worked for weekly wages and usually came and went on a daily basis.

====Synonyms====
* [[charlady]]
* [[Cinderella]] {{qualifier|disparaging}}
* [[cleaning woman]]

